devfreq: reimplement on mainline APIs

The vendor version needs rockchip_init_opp_table(), the system monitor
and the IPA power model, none of which exist upstream, so patch 0001
compiled it out and the NPU sat at one rate.

This version uses generic APIs only. The OPP core reads
operating-points-v2 and moves clock and regulator together. The driver
already tracks per-core busy time on a 1 s hrtimer for its debugfs load
file, so get_dev_status() feeds that to a stock governor. Rate changes
requested while the NPU is unpowered wait for the next power-on - the
clock and rail live inside the NPU power domains, and touching them
with the domain down wedges the interconnect.

Locking: devfreq holds devfreq->lock across target(), and the power paths
take their own power_lock, so target() must not take power_lock - the first
version did and deadlocked every devfreq sysfs read. It cannot use
power_refcount to decide whether the hardware is up either, because
rknpu_power_get() increments that before rknpu_power_on() enables the clocks
and resumes the domains, and it stays non-zero while power-off runs. Both
windows would put an SCMI rate change on unpowered hardware. It asks runtime
PM instead, which reports the real state and pins it for the duration.

Comes up on the userspace governor with polling off, so nothing retunes
the NPU until asked. Switch to simple_ondemand for load-based scaling.

Track the requested rate rather than reading it back. clk_get_rate() on
the SCMI clock returns the CRU-derived rate, not the PVTPLL rate the
firmware actually programmed, so it always reports 800 MHz however the
NPU is really clocked. Using it left current_freq permanently unequal to
the request, so every power-on re-ran a full rate and regulator
transition - hundreds per second under load, which hung the board.

Keep the vendor's POWER_DOWN_FREQ handling. BL31 owns the NPU PVTPLL and
its state does not survive the power domain going down: once devfreq has
moved off the boot rate, the next power cycle comes back up wedged and the
board hangs on the first job. Sustained inference at the boot rate is
completely stable - 300 runs, no transition - so it is the transition plus
a power cycle that is fatal, not the load. Dropping to a rate the CRU can
produce on its own before the domain powers down, and restoring the real
rate on resume, is what the vendor driver does; rk3528 expresses the same
requirement upstream as an opp-suspend.

Hold the NPU clocks locally across both runtime-PM callbacks rather than
inheriting them. rknpu_power_on() enables the bulk, resumes the domains and
only then calls pm_runtime_get_sync(), so the driver's own path leaves the
clocks up by the time the callbacks run - but nothing guarantees that entry
point. The PM core can resume the device on its own, and writing "on" to
power/control does exactly that. With PCLK_NPUTOP_ROOT gated, asking BL31
to reprogram PVTPLL writes into a dead APB and hangs the CPU:

  echo on > /sys/devices/platform/soc/27700000.npu/power/control

reboots the board on its own. The reproducer needs no governor switch, no
workload and no externally requested frequency transition: runtime resume
restores the SCMI-backed PVTPLL rate itself, and does so while the APB
those registers sit behind is gated. The request goes out even when the
rate being restored is the one already selected. The suspend side has the
same exposure, since it parks the SCMI clock at POWER_DOWN_FREQ.

Take a reference in the callbacks instead. The clock framework refcounts
nested enables, so this is safe when the normal power path already holds
the clocks. Restore the pending rate unconditionally while holding it, and
propagate what it returns: reporting a successful resume having skipped or
failed the restore would leave the PM core treating the device as fully
operational with a stale rate that nothing else would come back to apply.
A failed suspend likewise puts devfreq back rather than leaving it
suspended under a device the PM core still considers active.

Empty out rknpu_devfreq_lock() and rknpu_devfreq_unlock(). They guarded the
vendor's rockchip_opp_info through the Rockchip OPP framework's DVFS lock,
and this rewrite has no such state; an earlier version of this patch reached
for devfreq->lock as the nearest equivalent, which deadlocks. rknpu_drv.c
holds these across the whole of rknpu_power_on(), whose pm_runtime_get_sync()
enters runtime resume, and resume ends in devfreq_resume_device(). Under
simple_ondemand that calls devfreq_monitor_resume(), which takes
devfreq->lock on a task already holding it. The userspace governor never
showed it because its GOV_RESUME handler does not touch that mutex. lockdep:

  rknn_smoke/273 is trying to acquire lock:
    (&devfreq->lock) at: devfreq_monitor_resume+0x34/0x100
  but task is already holding lock:
    (&devfreq->lock) at: rknpu_devfreq_lock+0x20/0x30 [rknpu]

Serialise every rate change on one lock and one code path. The OPP call and
the clk_set_rate after it have to be a single step: a request landing between
them leaves the rail set for one point with the clock at another - 725 mV
holding up 900 MHz. The governor, a deferred request replayed on power-on and
the thermal cooling device all now go through npu_devfreq_apply_locked(),
which re-reads the request after applying it instead of counting attempts, so
a writer that lands mid-apply is neither lost nor left waiting for the next
power cycle.

Apply the pending rate for a caller that has just powered the hardware up.
pm_runtime_get_sync() runs no resume callback when the device is already
RPM_ACTIVE, which is the state writing "on" to power/control leaves behind, so
without rknpu_devfreq_sync_rate() a rate deferred while the SCMI gate was shut
would sit there unapplied.

Park unconditionally. The park is what stops the PVTPLL rate outliving the
power domain and wedging the SoC on the next job, so devfreq never having come
up is not a reason to skip it - only the devfreq bookkeeping around it is
conditional.


